STORY LENGTH: taste
SCENE DENSITY (CONTINUATION — AFTERMATH/CONSEQUENCE PACE): this scene follows a planner-committed event with real consequences still to land — it is NOT an opening/establishing beat and NOT an arc winding down. Give it FULL scene weight: real aftermath (who reacts, and how — family, witnesses, authority), institutional or social process where the story calls for it (a ruling, a summons, a date set, an argument over what happens next), enough DIALOGUE and INTERIORITY to carry it (this is not a declarative-only wrap-up — characters think, argue, and are changed by what just happened), and PLOT DIRECTION that moves the story somewhere new. End on a genuine NEW pressure point or choice the protagonist must now face — never on suspended mood alone ("waits", "holds its breath", "remains half-raised") with nothing concrete underneath it. No word-count ceiling applies to this scene — use the room a full scene actually needs.
LI PRESENCE FLOOR (HARD — Roman 2026-09-21): if Julian is present in this scene, Julian MUST have at least ONE meaningful moment that affects the scene — a line of dialogue, a visible/felt emotional reaction, a refusal, an intervention, a procedural move, or a charged silence the room reacts to. Julian may remain restrained and let another party (family, an authority, an antagonist) be the scene's public engine — this is NOT a requirement that Julian dominate the scene, rescue the protagonist, or speak more than once. It IS a requirement that Julian never simply stand in the room doing nothing while everyone else acts. A beat that only reports Julian's stillness or silence, with no consequence anyone in the room registers, does not satisfy this floor: the moment must change something — who speaks next, what the protagonist can still do, or what the room believes.
