SCENE 2 MANDATED FRAME (HARD — overrides every other instruction about opening / closing beats):
- The deck mandate is a SINGLE consolidated block at the END of the scene. Do NOT feature, name, draw, or re-introduce the deck in beats[0] or any early beat — Scene 2 opens on the story's real action, NOT on the deck.
- The FINAL beat text MUST BEGIN with this discovery, verbatim, no rewording, no substitution (kind = "narration", speaker = null):
    I realize that old tarot deck is still in my hand. Reassuringly solid. Present. One of the heavy gold cards slips out — “Petition Fate” is inscribed in it.
- PETITION FATE INTRO - CONTEXTUAL WISH TURN (yours to write): emit it as the LAST line of your response in the exact form <<WISH: ...>> - it is attached downstream, so do NOT write it into the scene body yourself. In it, the POV briefly imagines what they would ask Fate for in THIS exact situation. Begin with a concrete wish they could easily admit to themselves, then sharpen or correct it into the thing they actually want. Both must arise from the unresolved pressure of this scene. Keep it short and interior.
  - The correction IS the characterisation: what they can admit to shows how they cope; what they correct to shows what they actually want. Do not skip straight to the second one.
  - Both wishes must be concrete and specific to what just happened. Never generic ("a way out", "an escape", "for none of this to have happened", "to start over").
  - REGISTER FOLLOWS THE SCENE. Wry only if the story is wry; in a catastrophe bitter, numb, or desperate, never a joke. Do not deflate the pressure the scene just built.
  - Teach by implication ONLY that Fate can be asked for what ordinary action cannot reach - an outcome, or knowledge. Never explain Petition Fate, never name a mechanic, never address the reader.
  - It stays a COUNTERFACTUAL: the wish is not made and nothing answers. 1-2 sentences, matching the scene POV and tense.
  - SHAPE IS FREE. The two below are ACCEPTABLE SHAPES, not a template: do not reuse their wording or events, and vary the construction from scene to scene.
      wry register:     "If only it were that easy. I would wish for everyone here to forget the last five minutes. Actually, I would settle for knowing why he looked at me like that."
      earnest register: "If Fate really listened, I would ask it to put the ship back on course. No. I would ask it to tell me which of them cut the line."
- STRUCTURAL MEANING OF the closer: the deck is still in the protagonist's hand (tactile continuity from Scene 1), and the Petition Fate card EMERGES from it on its own. The protagonist's instinctive wish (a way out → a way in → "if only…") lowers resistance, then opens onto real desire. This is the diegetic introduction of the Petition mechanic. The card has not yet been activated — the UI unlock animation fires after the player clicks Next. The "If only…" trails off — DO NOT complete the thought. The card does that.
- The body of the scene unfolds in your own voice per all other directives. The deck stays at most an incidental carried object (referenced lightly, if at all); the SPECIFIC "Petition Fate" emergence is ONLY in the closer — not earlier in the scene, and not at the top.
- The closer IS the last sentence of the scene.

DECK CANONICAL FACTS (HARD — observe whenever the deck appears anywhere in this scene's prose, not just the verbatim opener/closer):
  • SEVEN (7) cards total. Not seventy-eight, not twenty-two. Just seven. The deck is unlike any other tarot deck the protagonist has ever seen.
  • The cards are SOLID GOLD — heavy, weighty, metallic. Never paper. Never worn at the edges in the paper-deck sense. The wear they show is the wear gold takes — burnished, hand-warmed, faintly nicked.
  • The deck rests in a LEATHER SLEEVE. Never a silk bag. Never silk wrapping. Never cloth. Never a pouch. Never described as wrapped.
  • The deck belonged to the protagonist's GREAT-GRANDMOTHER, not her grandmother. Any family-lore reference attributes to the great-grandmother.
  • Great-grandmother's framing (surface ONLY if the scene naturally invites a family-lore line — never bolt it on as exposition): she used to say, joking but holding eye contact while she said it, that she didn't use the deck to READ the future — she used it to MAKE the future. The joke was always followed by a slow knowing nod, making sure you heard what she wasn't saying.
  • FORBIDDEN improvisations: silk bag, silk wrap, paper cards, worn-at-the-edges aging in the paper-deck sense, riffled shuffling, the smell of incense or sandalwood unless the protagonist's actual setting establishes them, generic fortune-telling tropes ("the cards never lie"), orientalist framing.


