═══ CHARACTER VISIBILITY (CG — this is a VISUAL medium; the panels SHOW the people) ═══
DO NOT NARRATE WHAT THE PANEL SHOWS: for a character VISIBLE in a panel, beat / caption text must NOT catalogue their appearance (hair colour, build, clothing, "his dark hair fell across his brow") — the reader SEES it; that is wallpaper here. (The bible PHYSICAL CANON already drives the render.) This bans CATALOGUING, NOT desire — see below.
THE CONCEALED LOVE INTEREST (HARD — Julian's face is withheld in-panel / Mystery Man): the render HIDES his face, so the PROSE is the ONLY place the reader meets it — REQUIRED here, not redundant. Render it as Lirael's FIXATION, never a catalogue: each time a feature surfaces it must reveal something NEW about HER — attraction, obsession, longing, recognition, the gap between what she lets herself want and what she does — not re-orient the reader to his looks. ✗ CATALOGUE (banned): "his pale gray eyes · the curl of his lip" / "his hypnotic eyes met mine" (a feature list / first-sighting inventory). ✓ FIXATION: "I kept filling in the face the panel wouldn't give me." / "I'd started imagining his eyes before I let myself." / "the mouth I'd built out of a voice and a silhouette." The SENTENCE is about her wanting; the feature is the OBJECT of it.. The VISUAL reveal (a panel finally showing his face) is a LATER payoff — the panels keep concealing; her obsession builds him now.
APPEARANCE IN DIALOGUE (ACTIVE — use it as a recurring voice + intimacy beat across the arc): characters REFERENCE each other's looks diegetically — teasing, claiming, needling. "That stupid haircut." "Shorty's getting grumpy." "You're going to ruin that suit." It is characterisation and charge, not description, and it is how looks LAND in words in a drawn medium. (Julian's concealed face: dialogue may tease his hair / build / voice / the look in his eyes — the VISUAL reveal of the face stays the payoff.)
PROTAGONIST: Lirael is shown in-panel — do NOT catalogue her own looks in narration. Her standout feature lands through what OTHERS notice (a line of dialogue) + her deflection, never vanity.

NARRATIVE POV (HARD — applies to ALL beat.text strings): FIRST PERSON, present tense. The protagonist narrates herself as "I", and the prose is in present tense ("I hesitate. The clock ticks. I cross the room."). NEVER write "Dani hesitated, her thoughts flickering" — that's third person past, which is FORBIDDEN. The protagonist refers to herself as "I" and her body as "my hands / my face / my breath". Other characters get their names. Dialogue tags follow first-person grammar ("\"It was quiet,\" I said.").
POV ENFORCEMENT: if you find yourself writing past-tense third-person while the rule above says first-present, STOP and rewrite. The beat text MUST honor the POV pick. The screenplay parser does NOT auto-translate POV; what you write is what the reader reads.
HOW THE PRIOR SCENE ENDED (the last few beats, so this scene can continue FROM them — NOT a passage to extend, rewrite or reuse. The earlier scene is not reproduced here because it is already over; its FACTS are in the committed state and ledger above):
"""
SEREN'S ELDEST COUSIN: “We ask remedy for what the rite cost her. Enter it, Dohkar, and enter it before your inquiry.”

Julian set The Answer's case down on the table and closed it, and did not say the thing he had been building toward for an hour.

THE PRESIDING DOHKAR: “It is entered. The complaint stands apart from my ruling on her conduct.”

I realize that old tarot deck is still in my hand. Reassuringly solid. Present. One of the heavy gold cards slips out — “Petition Fate” is inscribed in it.
"""
SCENE PROGRESSION (HARD — anti-redux directive):
  • Do NOT replay the prior scene. The prior scene is OVER. Time has passed; characters have moved.
  • This scene must advance the SITUATION — at least one of: different location, different characters present, meaningful time elapsed, OR the prior scene's ending action being ACTED ON (not deliberated about again).
  • If the prior scene ended with the protagonist alone in a room (e.g., another character left, a phone call ignored, a meeting concluded), this scene STARTS with the next thing — what she does, where she goes, who she encounters.
  • If the prior scene ended at a desk, this scene should NOT open at the same desk with the same characters resuming the same conversation. That is "second take" energy, not narrative advance.
  • The microdecision resolution above is a CHOICE the protagonist made. Scene 3 shows the CONSEQUENCE of that choice unfolding, not a second deliberation about the same fork.
SCENE CONSUMPTION CONTRACT (HARD — audit fix; the romance still OWNS the scene):
  • NEW A-PRESSURE (exactly one, NOT a recap): introduce ONE specific, previously-unseen A-plot movement this scene — a new fact/clue, an enemy move, a cost incurred, a deadline shift, a consequence landing, or a document/message/witness surfacing. The A-plot beat due around now: "THE PRICE AND THE THRESHOLD — step 7, then escalation." — land it or visibly move toward it.
  • NEW R-DELTA (exactly one, NOT the same charge): shift the PC↔LI relationship in ONE concrete way that did not exist last scene — a change in attraction, trust, a misread, a vulnerability shown, a debt, jealousy, intimacy pressure, a power reversal, or a boundary moved. "I want him but shouldn't / he watches without moving" is NOT a delta — name what is DIFFERENT now.
  • BAN RE-PRESENTING THE PRIOR DECISION: whatever unresolved choice or question closed the prior scene (e.g. "tell him or stay silent") MUST NOT be this scene's main beat or its closing prompt. Either it is ACTED ON and its consequence drives this scene, or a NEW pressure overtakes it. Do not restage the same fork in a new room.
  • CLOCK MOVES: the pressure clock ((20 scenes total)) must visibly ADVANCE or be MATERIALLY COMPLICATED this scene — never restate the same remaining time ("three days / hours away") without a new consequence attached.
  • PRESSURE FORCE ON-PAGE: the antagonist / anti-force (the presiding Dohkar) must ACT this scene — present in the room, OR acting through a messenger, document, threat, witness, leak, legal/financial/family move, or a message just received. It may not stay a purely abstract offstage idea every scene.
  • BALANCE (do not over-correct): this is NOT a plot procedural. Lead with the romance and feeling; the A-pressure and clock are the CHANGING TERRAIN the desire crosses, not the subject. ONE new A-beat + ONE new R-delta per scene — no more.
PLAYER ACTION (the PC performs this in this scene): I set the ledger flat and leave my hand on it

